version deprecated
	Configure an RFC 3984 / H.264 packetiser for a VidMP[tx]
apifn	sm_vidmptx_config_codec_rfc3984
params	sm_vidmptx_codec_rfc3984_parms codecp
	The VidMP[tx] to which to add the packetiser
field	tSMVidMPtxId vidmptx in
	The payload type identifer to use with this packetiser (see
	<a href="http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc3550.txt">IETF RFC 3550</a>
	section 13). Supplying a value of
	<code>-1</code> will remove any payload type configuration from the 
	packetiser preventing its use.
field	tSM_INT payload_type in
	Allows behaviour with aggregation packets to be modified. If this parameter
	is set to 0, each aggregation packet received as input is transmitted as a
	whole. Otherwise, each such packet is split and one packet transmitted for
	each H.264 NALU. Setting this to a value other than 0 should not be necessary
	when transmitting to an	endpoint which is properly RFC3984 compliant.
field	tSM_INT deaggregate in
unstruct
	Configures an RFC 3984 packetiser, as defined in
	<a href="http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc3984.txt">IETF RFC 3984</a>,
	to the VidMP[tx] setting the payload type mapping to
ref	payload_type.
	<p>
	The packetiser accepts Audio Video Format (AVF) frames from the
	VidMP[tx] incoming datafeed as input, and composes RTP packets as
	output.
	<p>
	A newly configured packetiser will not emit any RTP packets until it
	receives an H.264 I-Frame in the incoming AVF stream. Waiting for an
	I-Frame attempts to ensure that the video, when rendered by the remote
	endpoint, starts cleanly and without the picture disruption which will
	occur were the rendering decoder to attempt to decode P-Frames (Predicted)
	without an I-Frame as reference.
	<p>
	The packetiser will emit H.264 parameter sets over RTP whenever received
	in the incoming AVF stream. In practice, this means it will emit parameter
	sets every I-frame. Although the parameter sets are unlikely to change
	often, transmitting them with each I-frame allows more robustness against
	packet loss, the ability for a remote endpoint to start decoding on any
	I-frame (i.e. mid-stream) and appears to be the norm amongst videophones.
	<p>
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error
